iscusses some authors in a bit  of a digression  who have what she's thinking of as mixed  styles where they their their work is  heroic and comic at the same time and  when it's heroic they speak in a heroic  way but when it's comic they talk in the  ordinary parlance of you know whatever  time period they're writing uh  you know rogers zelazny here we're  thinking perhaps of the chronicles of  amber  for its liber we could think of the  fawford and the grey mouser stuff as  well as many others she also mentions  jack vance but she says vance doesn't do  quite the same  thing as them and she says fritz liber  and rogers of the lassie lazy both  written in the comic heroic vein  their technique is to alternate when  humor is intended they speak colloquial  american english or even slaying right  readers indifferent to language do not  mind this but for others the strain is  too great i am one of those latter so  it's interesting to see her you know  zelazny and leguin are part of the same  time and and movements often called the  new wave of you know science fiction and  fantasy and here she's she's criticizing  zelazny for that she says it's strange  because  both libra and zelazny are skillful and  highly imaginative writers and it's  perfectly clear that libra  profoundly acquainted with shakespeare  in practice in a very broad range of  techniques could maintain any tone with  eloquence and grace  sometimes i wonder if these two  underestimate their own talents or they  lack confidence in themselves or it may  be that since fantasy is seldom taken  seriously at this particular era they  are afraid to take it seriously  themselves and so their humor becomes  self-mocking and self-destructive their  gods and heroes keep turning aside to  look out of the book at you and whisper  see we're just really plain folks  and she goes on and talks about vance he  says his humor is so quiet you can miss  it if you blink the whole tone of his  writing is so modest sometimes i